{# Prompt v2. Differs from v1 in exactly one respect: the model is shown the
   COMPLETE file it is asked to diff against, instead of the enclosing method
   plus the class name.

   v1 asks for a unified diff with hunk context against the whole file, while
   showing only a method body and a bare class name. The context lines the
   format requires were therefore never in the prompt, so the model had to
   guess them -- and the content-anchored applier refuses a hunk whose context
   does not match, which is the correct behaviour. The result was a large,
   silent patch-application failure rate across unrelated vendors, and it is
   the confound this version exists to remove.

   No line-number gutter, deliberately: rules/diffapply.py anchors each hunk by
   content and treats the @@ offset as a hint only, so numbers would buy no
   correctness while adding a fresh way to fail (a model that copies the gutter
   into its diff). The file is shown exactly as it is on disk, so every context
   line is quotable verbatim.

   Everything else -- policy rendering, rule list, feedback block, output
   contract -- is held byte-identical to v1 on purpose, so a v1/v2 comparison
   isolates context scope and nothing else. Do not "improve" the wording here
   without forking a v3; drift would silently destroy that comparison. #}
